  "worked_solution": "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-5x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x-6)(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-6)(x+1)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-1,6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=6$ and $x=-1$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/assets/b3d4116e1157d2f9b5414a015965b371982663518d04631810f19d8be4a7de93.png)",
